# There must be exactly one syscall for consequences

Think about Linux.

Arbitrary processes do not write straight to the hard disk controller.

They cross the kernel boundary.

Do that here.

Anything that:

```text
spends money
publishes publicly
sends a message
changes a listing
creates an ad
orders goods
changes DNS
moves funds
```

must enter:

```python
runtime.execute(action_spec)
```

And no other path should be able to cause that effect.

That is how you make qprivately real rather than ceremonial.

